{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>Observe and you shall hear.</b> It is difficult, for it should have said ״שְׁמַע וְשָׁמַרְתָּ״, so why did it put שְׁמִירָה before שְׁמִיעָה? Further, what does the word <b>אֵת</b> include, and what does the word <b>כָּל</b> include? Further, why did it speak of the good of the children only in this verse? Further, one must understand its saying <b>כִּי תַעֲשֶׂה הַטּוֹב</b>, which appears to give a reason for the statement ״לְמַעַן יִיטַב״ — but it has already preceded and said <b>שְׁמֹר</b> etc., and finished saying <b>לְמַעַן</b> etc.; behold, the good mentioned there is already the reward for the שְׁמִירָה and שְׁמִיעָה that the verse stated before it." "Now the intention is that it commands concerning two things: (1) Torah and the commandments of HaShem, which were already made known to Israel from the mouth of Moshe, and (2) the fences and safeguards that our Rabbis of blessed memory innovated. Therefore, corresponding to Torah and commandments that are already known from the Torah, it said <b>שְׁמֹר</b>; and corresponding to matters that are innovated by our Rabbis of blessed memory, it said <b>וְשָׁמַעְתָּ</b> — that one must accept every decree of our Rabbis of blessed memory and their safeguards. And this is what it alluded to in the statement <b>אֵת כָּל</b>: the meaning of 'אֵת' is to include all the decrees, protections, and fences that our Rabbis of blessed memory innovated; and its saying <b>כָּל</b> means that through the fences the matters will be kept in completeness — and this is its saying <b>כָּל הַדְּבָרִים</b>, for without this the commandment is not fulfilled in its entirety, since through the fence nothing is detracted from it. And it concludes saying <b>לְמַעַן יִיטַב לְךָ וּלְבָנֶיךָ</b>, for the fences and safeguards that our Rabbis of blessed memory made are a thing that sustains the generations of the people of Israel forever, to separate them from all ugliness and its like." "And its saying <b>כִּי תַעֲשֶׂה הַטּוֹב</b> etc. — meaning: for the matters that the Sages decree are nothing but to do the good and the right in the eyes of HaShem, for all their words are a golden thread. And according to this, the statement <b>כִּי תַעֲשֶׂה</b> etc. is also the reason for the commandment of 'וְשָׁמַעְתָּ', for through this you will do the good and the right that HaShem commanded, by means of the fences and the safeguards — and this is besides the good attained for the generations through their words of blessed memory. And according to this approach, its saying <b>כִּי תַעֲשֶׂה</b> is an additional reason for the statement 'וְשָׁמַעְתָּ.'" "<b>Further,</b> the verse alludes in the statement <b>שְׁמֹר וְשָׁמַעְתָּ</b> that a person should not say: ״Why do I need to learn this halakhah after I have already grasped it and know what I must observe in this commandment?״ Scripture comes to say ״שְׁמֹר״ — meaning, even after you have observed, and from what follows you know that you have already stood upon the matters to know what to observe — nevertheless <b>וְשָׁמַעְתָּ</b>, meaning, you are not exempt from engaging in the oral learning of the commandment." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained on the basis of its saying (Hosea 14:3) ״וּנְשַׁלְּמָה פָרִים שְׂפָתֵינוּ״. Now since it said ״שְׁמֹר״ etc. ״אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים״, and this matter falls under the category of the impossible, for a person is able to fulfill all the matters — therefore it said <b>שְׁמֹר</b> — meaning, corresponding to negative commandments, which are in a person's power to guard himself from all negative commandments; and corresponding to positive commandments it said <b>וְשָׁמַעְתָּ</b> — meaning, he should fulfill the commandments through oral study of their law by mouth, and through this it is accounted for you as though it came to your hand and you fulfilled it; and from now on one can keep and do all the matters." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 19a), who said: a person should recite [the text] and then explain it. And this is the verse's saying <b>shemor ve-shamata</b> — meaning, shemor: that he should preserve knowledge of the halakhah, which is the girsa (recitation), and shamata — which is the sevara (reasoning), to sharpen his understanding of what he has heard with his intellect." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the way of their words, of blessed memory (Zohar vol. III, p. 193a), that for one who is a master of transgressions, the gates of the Torah's knowledge are locked before him. And you will find (Shulchan Aruch of the Ari z\"l, Laws of Talmud Torah) that the gates of the entrances to understanding the words of the Torah are the difficulties that confront a person, and they are the sod of the kelipot that form from a person's transgressions. And this is what the verse alerted in this statement <b>shemor ve-shamata</b> — meaning, if you wish to stand on the truth of the Torah to understand its sayings, observe the commandments so as not to transgress them, and thereby \"and you shall hear all,\" etc." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the way of their words, of blessed memory (Chagigah 15b), and this is their language: \"If the sage is like an angel of YHWH of Hosts, seek Torah from his mouth,\" etc., end of quote. And this is the verse's saying <b>shemor</b> — meaning, if he is in observance, that he observes the Torah, then \"you shall hear\" Torah from his mouth. And the verse's saying <b>et kol ha-devarim</b>, along this path, may be explained according to what they said in Chagigah (there), and this is their language: \"And how did Rabbi Meir learn Torah from the mouth of Aḥer? But was it not stated, etc., 'For the lips of a priest' (Malachi 2:7), etc.? Resh Lakish said: Rabbi Meir found a verse and expounded it: 'Incline your ear' (Isaiah 55:3), etc., 'and set your heart to My knowledge' — it was not said 'to their knowledge' but 'to My knowledge,' etc. The verses contradict one another! No contradiction: this refers to a great person, this refers to a minor.\" And they said further there, and this is their language: \"Rava expounded: What is written 'Unto a walnut garden' (Song of Songs 6:11), etc.? Rabba bar Shila found Elijah; he said to him: What is the Holy One, blessed be He, doing? He said to him: He cites teachings from the mouths of all the sages, but from the mouth of Rabbi Meir He does not cite, because he learned teachings from the mouth of Aḥer. He said to him: Rabbi Meir found a pomegranate, etc. He said to him: Now He says 'Meir My son says,' etc.,\" end of quote. Behold, according to their words, of blessed memory, a great person may learn from the sage even if the sage is a wicked person, unlike a minor. And the Rambam, of blessed memory — we find that he wrote (Laws of Talmud Torah, ch. 4) that one may not learn from a sage unless he is like an angel of YHWH, and he did not distinguish between great and minor, according to the words of the Gemara." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Ba\"ch (Shach, Yoreh De'ah, sec. 246) who wrote that the Gemara distinguished only according to the opinion of Rabbi Meir, but the Talmud does not hold so, and one must examine the language of the Talmud which says \"Rabbi Meir found a verse and expounded it\" — implying that specifically Rabbi Meir is the one who holds thus, end of quote." "And his words are not correct. First, whence does the Talmud have the authority to say that the act of Rabbi Meir stands as a dispute, such that we can draw any reasoning from it at all? Furthermore, from the fact that it raises a difficulty for Rabbi Meir from the exposition of \"For the lips of a priest,\" it is proven that it wishes to equate them — for if you say that Aḥer's truth, that Rabbi Meir comes to distinguish, is from the exposition which teaches about a great person, if so, what is the Talmud's difficulty from the exposition of \"For the lips of a priest,\" etc., if not that you must say he holds this exposition is agreed upon, for if not, he could have answered that Rabbi Meir does not expound thus. And if you say this is the intent of the responder who said \"Rabbi Meir found a verse,\" etc., meaning he does not agree with the exposition of \"lips of a priest,\" etc., if so, what does it come back to say \"The verses contradict one another,\" etc., if this expositor does not expound that one? Furthermore, behold Rava, who is a later amora, expounded \"Unto a walnut garden\" — just as this walnut, even though it is muddied with mud, etc., the food inside it is not rendered repugnant, etc., end of quote — and these words are like the reasoning of Rabbi Meir, and it is known that the halakhah follows Rava even though it is said that the act of Rabbi Meir is disputed. Furthermore, if the exposition of \"For the lips of a priest\" applies both to a great person and to a minor and only Rabbi Meir alone holds to distinguish, what does Rabba bar Shila answer Elijah, \"Rabbi Meir found a pomegranate,\" etc.? Does he not hold this according only to Rabbi Meir, whereas the Talmud holds that there is no license to learn except from one who is like an angel, whether great or minor? Furthermore, what greater proof is there than this, that HaShem agreed to the act of Rabbi Meir, for He went back and cited a teaching in his name. And if you say: at first the Holy One, blessed be He, what did He hold, and in the end what did He hold — for one cannot say that the reason that Rabba bar Shila stated, \"Rabbi Meir found a pomegranate,\" etc., was hidden from the eyes of HaShem, perish the thought! Know that HaShem holds a person to account for doing something that is not pure except from HaShem and not from man, and it is reckoned to him as a sin until he is pure both from HaShem and from man. Therefore, when Rabbi Meir did this act — learning from a person who is not like an angel — Rabbi Meir was pure before HaShem but not before man, as long as his reason was not known, whether his deed was pure and upright. And this was the reason HaShem did not say a teaching in his name, because of his lacking in perfection. But after it was stated in the heavenly academy a reason for praise in his deeds, he entered the category of \"you shall be clean also from Israel\" (Numbers 32:22), and therefore immediately HaShem went back and cited the teaching in his name. And the ultimate truth is according to the words of Rabbi Meir." "<b>And the correct approach</b> to give a reason for the Rambam is this: he holds that in this era there is no mind so complete that it may be called גדול (great) to permit him to learn from a wicked scholar, since specifically Rabbi Meir, who was complete in mind and knowledge, was the one to whom this was permitted — for there is no concern that he would surely learn his wickedness from him. And perhaps it is for this reason that the Talmud makes its precise wording and says: רבי מאיר קרא אשכח ודרש וכו׳, רבי מאיר רימון וכו׳ (Rabbi Meir found a verse and expounded it… Rabbi Meir [was like] a pomegranate…) — meaning, specifically this fence of Rabbi Meir is what exists in this permissibility, because he would certainly cast away the klipah and eat only the food. And this reasoning is correct and self-evident; therefore the Rambam stated unequivocally the prohibition against learning from a teacher who is not like an angel, whether for a great person or a small one, for the generations have diminished and we do not have this permissibility except in the case of Rabbi Meir and his like. And go forth and learn from their words, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 112b), who said: if the earlier ones were like angels…" "<b>And on</b> this basis it will be explained correctly what the verse says: <b>Observe and hear all these things</b> etc. — meaning, when do I impose upon you the condition that you may not hear Torah teaching except from a person who guards the Torah and the commandments? Only for one who is small and lacking, who needs to hear all the matters or the majority of them — and the majority is like the whole. But the great person, who needs to hear only a small portion — he may hear even from a person who does not guard Torah and commandments, like Rabbi Meir, who would learn Torah from another even though that other did not guard Torah and commandments, for he would cast away the klipah and eat its interior."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {4 "The phrase גֶּדֶר הַנִּמְנָע literally means 'the category of the impossible'; it is translated hyper-literally as 'the impossible' to preserve the text as written, though the sense is that fulfilling 'all the matters' borders on the unattainable without the mechanism the verse now provides."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:52.354202507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}